==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|abutt}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈbʌt/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-abut.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈbʌt/|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈbʊt/|a=Northern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌt|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|abutten}}, from {{der|en|ML.|abuttare}} and {{der|en|fro|abuter}}, {{m|fro|aboter}}, {{m|fro|abouter||to touch at one end, to come to an end, aim, reach}},<ref name=WI3/><ref name=RHD>{{R:RHCD|page=7}}</ref> from {{der|en|fro|but||end, aim, purpose}}; akin to {{cog|non|butr||piece of wood}}.<ref name=WI3/> Equivalent to {{prefix|en|a|butt|t1=to|t2=boundary mark}}.<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=11}}</ref>

====Verb====
{{en-verb|++}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[touch]] by means of a mutual [[border]], [[edge]] or [[end]]; to [[border]] on; to lie [[adjacent]] (to); to be [[contiguous]] (said of an area of land) {{defdate|First attested around 1350 to 1470.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#: {{ux|en|It was a time when Germany still '''abutted''' upon Russia.}}
#: {{ux|en|His land '''abuts''' on the road.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To border upon; be next to; [[abut on]]; be adjacent to. {{defdate|First attested in the mid-19<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>

=====Usage notes=====
* Followed by any of the following words: '''upon''', '''[[abut on|on]]''' or {{qualifier|obsolete}} '''to'''.<ref name=WI3/><ref name=SOED/>

=====Derived terms=====
{{col|en|abutment|abuttal|abutter}}

